    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've written `clean_orders(df)` in `app/clean.py`. The project's one test passes (`pytest -q`), and so did a temporary test I wrote to check the cleaning itself.\n\n**What it does:**\n- It works on a copy (`df.copy()`), so the input frame isn't changed. The temporary test confirmed this.\n- **Text columns:** it strips leading and trailing whitespace and turns empty or all-whitespace strings into missing values. This works for both of pandas 3's text column types:\n  - **The newer `str` type** (what `load_orders` produces): the column type stays the same.\n  - **Plain `object` columns:** only string values are changed. Numbers and existing missing values in the same column are left alone.\n- **`qty`:** missing values are filled with 0. Everything else, including `price`, is unchanged.\n\n**What I checked:** the temporary test fed a CSV through `load_orders` with padded values (like `\" A1 \"`) and blank fields, plus an `object` column mixing strings, numbers and blanks. The results matched the rules above.\n\n**Decision for you:** `qty` stays a decimal (float) column after the fill, because pandas makes it one when the CSV has blank quantities. I didn't convert it to whole numbers because you asked to leave other values as they are. If you want whole numbers, I can add `.astype(\"int64\")`, though it would fail if a quantity has a fractional part.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** my permissions didn't allow deleting the temporary test, so the `scratch/` folder (holding `test_scratch.py`) is still there. It's safe to delete. Plain `pytest -q` doesn't run it because it only looks in `tests/`."
  },
